The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-6x^2+x-1}{x^2+3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-6x^2+x-1}{x^{2}}}{\dfrac{x^2+3}{x^{2}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-6\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x}-\dfrac{\cancel 1}{ x^2}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-6+\dfrac{1}{x}-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}{1+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-6+0-0}{1+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-6
\end{align}$
